WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:04.590
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:09.220
Not so long ago, we talked about a technique
that enabled us to render stunningly high

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:12.110
quality cloth models in real-time.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:16.990
It supported level of detail, self shadowing,
and lots of other goodies that make computer

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:21.239
game developers, and of course, my humble
self super happy.

00:00:21.239 --> 00:00:25.960
And today, we're going to talk about a technique
that is able to create these highly detailed

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:28.970
cloth geometries for our digital characters.

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:34.640
I have really fond memories of attending to
the talk of the Oscar award winner Steve Marschner

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:40.100
on this paper a few years ago in Switzerland,
and I remember being so spellbound by it that

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.910
I knew this was a day I will never forget.

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:44.850
I am sure you'll love it too.

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:50.320
In this piece of work, the goal is to create
a digital garment model that is as detailed

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.370
and realistic as possible.

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:57.589
We start out with an input 3D geometry that
shows the rough shape of the model, then,

00:00:57.589 --> 00:01:00.489
we pick a knitting pattern of our choice.

00:01:00.489 --> 00:01:05.820
After that, the points of this knitting pattern
are moved so that they correctly fit this

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:08.630
3D geometry that we specified.

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:10.680
And now comes the coolest part!

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:16.110
What we created so far is an ad-hoc model
that doesn't really look and behave like a

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:17.790
real piece of cloth.

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:23.300
To remedy this, a physics-based simulation
is run that takes this ad-hoc model and the

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:28.420
output of this process will be a realistic
rest shape for these yarn curves.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:34.380
And here you can witness how the simulated
forces pull the entire piece of garment together.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:40.080
We start out with dreaming up a piece of cloth
geometry, and this simulator gradually transforms

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.680
it into a real-world version of that.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:46.440
This is a step that we call yarn-level relaxation.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:47.950
Wow.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:53.530
These final results not only look magnificent,
but in a physical simulation, they also behave

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:55.180
like real garments.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:57.790
It's such a joy to look at results like this.

00:01:57.790 --> 00:01:58.790
Loving it.

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:03.020
Again, I would like to note that we're not
talking about the visualization of the garment,

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:06.430
but creating a realistic piece of geometry.

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:11.140
The most obvious drawback of this technique
is its computation time - it was run on a

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:16.360
very expensive system and still took several
hours of number crunching to get this done.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:21.140
However, I haven't seen an implementation
of this on the graphics card yet, so if someone

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:26.030
can come up with an efficient way to do it,
in an ideal case, we may be able to do this

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:27.150
in several minutes.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:32.450
I also have to notify you about the fact that
it is considered a crime not having a look

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:34.420
at the paper in the video description.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:39.440
It does not suffice to say that it is well
written, it is so brilliantly presented, it's

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.510
truly a one of kind work that everyone has
to see.

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:48.110
If you enjoyed this episode, make sure to
subscribe to Two Minute Papers, we'd be happy

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:50.960
to have you in our growing club of Fellow
Scholars.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:54.880
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

